  "poe": {
    "budgetW": 500,
    "sourceUrl": "https://techspecs.ui.com/unifi/switching/us-48-500w",
    "note": "500 W, the vendor's own aggregate row: \"Max. PoE Output = Up to PoE+ / Total PoE Availability = 500W\" beside \"Port Layout = 1 GbE RJ45 48 (All PoE+) (1G/100M/10M)\" and \"PoE Ports = PoE+ 48\" (https://techspecs.ui.com/unifi/switching/us-48-500w, HTTP 200, read 2026-11-21). Previously parsed from the model name."
  },
  "notes": "Census from the QSG that covers both 48-port PoE models (Model: US-48-500W, US-48-750W; https://dl.ui.com/qsg/US-48-500W_US-48-750W/US-48-500W_US-48-750W_EN.html, HTTP 200, 19,990 B, read 2026-11-21): Networking \"(48) 10/100/1000 Mbps RJ45 Ethernet Ports / (2) 1/10 Gbps SFP+ Ethernet Ports / (2) 1 Gbps SFP Ethernet Ports\"; Management \"(1) RJ45 Serial Port Out-of-Band, Ethernet In-Band\"; PoE Per Port \"POE+ IEEE 802.3af/at (Pins 1, 2+; 3, 6-) / Max. PoE+ Wattage per Port by PSE 34.2W / Voltage Range 802.3at Mode 50-57V\"; the same page prints \"RJ45 ports support Power over Ethernet (PoE) and 10/100/1000 Ethernet connections\" for ports 1-48. The tech-spec page (https://techspecs.ui.com/unifi/switching/us-48-500w, HTTP 200, read 2026-11-21) independently prints \"10G SFP+ 2 (10G/1G)\" and \"1G SFP 2 (1G)\". This pass fixed two defects. (1) All 48 copper sockets carried `poeOut: true`, the bare direction bit: engine/media.js `poeDraw()` sums it as 1 W and the faceplate's `num()` prints nothing, so twenty 25.5 W AP-515s cabled here reported 20 W of draw against a 500 W budget and `pseDraw()` could never raise E_POE_BUDGET; each socket now declares the QSG's 34.2 W PSE ceiling with `poeOutClass: \"PoE+\"`, and 802.3af is declared beside 802.3at because the same row prints \"IEEE 802.3af/at\". (2) The model carried only the two 1G SFP cages - 50 sockets for 52 physical - so the 10G SFP+ pair was uncabled and unlabelled; `sfp+1`/`sfp+2` (10G/1G) are added in panel order ahead of the SFP pair, and the RJ45 serial console the QSG's Management row names is added as `console`. 34.2 W is the per-port PSE ceiling, not the whole-box allocation: the 500 W figure stays in `poe.budgetW` from the vendor's Total PoE Availability row."
}
